# A billion iterations, with a `mod` in the body.
#
# `mod` is here because it is the operation most easily got wrong: it has a
# question in front of it — dividing by zero stops — and answering that
# question with a call instead of a compare once cost twenty times a machine's
# own remainder, because a call is something the optimiser cannot see through.
# If this ever goes back to being several times `loop.c`, that is what has
# happened.
#
# The sum is written with `mod` rather than as a plain series so that nothing
# can work the answer out ahead of time instead of running the loop.

START {
    var.mut.int64 'total' = [*0*];
    loop.range.int64 'i' = [*1*, *1000000000*] {
        set 'total' = ['total' + ('i' mod *7*)];
    }
    print.stdout[str:*total * 'total' \n];
}
